## Working with {{language}}

Here's how to use string formatting in {{language}}:

Single braces: {variable} is plain literal text in v0.4.0 — single `{` / `}`
are never interpolated, so no escape is needed to suppress them.

```python
name = "World"
print(f"Hello {name}!")  # This should NOT be interpolated
result = {"key": "value"}  # This should NOT be interpolated
```

After the code block, interpolation resumes: {{language}} is great!

Another code block with template syntax:

```
@if condition:
This is literal text inside a code fence.
@end
{this_is_also_literal}
```

Back to normal: {{language}} rocks.

The v0.4.0 escape for a literal double-opening-brace in output is a leading
backslash. The fenced block below shows source and result side-by-side (both
are verbatim inside the fence — no processing occurs):

~~~
source:  \{{language}}
output:  {{language}}
~~~

Live — the line below uses the escape in source; the compiled output is the
literal text shown:

\{{language}} — the backslash-escape produced a literal `\{{` pair here.
